cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use sequential-analysis in your research, please cite it as below.
title: "sequential-analysis: sequential statistical inference separated from the experimental runtime"
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Alexander
    family-names: Stepanov
    affiliation: Sobolev Institute of Mathematics
  - given-names: Andrej
    family-names: Novikov
    affiliation: Novikov Laboratories LLC
version: 0.4.0
date-released: "2026-10-16"
license: AGPL-3.0-only
repository-code: "https://github.com/andre-a-no/sequential-analysis"
url: "https://pypi.org/project/sequential-analysis/"
abstract: >-
  Sequential testing and online statistical inference in Python: Wald's
  SPRT and Kiefer-Weiss optimal tests, anytime-valid A/B tests and
  confidence sequences (parametric and nonparametric), group sequential
  designs, A/B tests with false discovery rate control, change-point
  detection (CUSUM, Shiryaev-Roberts, nonparametric e-detectors, Bayesian
  online change points), and Kalman, particle and hidden Markov filters
  with smoothing; a TypeScript executor continues the same runs. Each
  procedure is a state-transition function F(S, X, R) -> (S', O, R'),
  separated from a reproducible runtime with checkpoints, replay and
  conformance vectors. Dual-licensed: GNU AGPL v3, or a commercial license
  from Novikov Laboratories LLC (see COMMERCIAL.md).
keywords:
  - sequential analysis
  - sequential testing
  - SPRT
  - A/B testing
  - anytime-valid inference
  - confidence sequences
  - nonparametric statistics
  - group sequential design
  - clinical trials
  - Kiefer-Weiss problem
  - change-point detection
  - Kalman filter
  - particle filter
  - hidden Markov model
  - reproducibility
